---
// Part 3 — copy from the Linear tickets (ADA-56 to 67). Three choices, one per
// level of the movement; hovering one widens it and brings up its photograph.
// Each opens its section below: a 70ch column broken up by full-bleed <Scene>s
// (credits in src/assets/story/README.md).
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import ReadProgress from '../components/ReadProgress.astro';
// You's photo is the licensed iStock already used on the homepage (iStock-2221195931)
import tea from '../assets/story/home-part-tea.jpg';
import community from '../assets/story/community-06-we-the-people-of.jpg';
import council from '../assets/story/country-v2-01-council.jpg';
import councilTalk from '../assets/story/country-v2-02-council-talk.jpg';
import impact from '../assets/story/country-v2-03-cultural-impact.jpg';
import policy from '../assets/story/country-v2-04-peoples-policy.jpg';
// the level panels' photos (ADA-56)
import communityPanel from '../assets/story/level-v2-community.jpg';
import countryPanel from '../assets/story/level-v2-country.jpg';

const LEVELS = [
  {
    id: 'you',
    n: 'I',
    lines: ['You'],
    teaser: 'Start with a 10-minute cup of tea.',
    image: tea,
  },
  {
    id: 'community',
    n: 'II',
    lines: ['Your', 'Community'],
    teaser: 'Hold a Council of the People where you live.',
    image: communityPanel,
    focus: '50% 55%',
  },
  {
    id: 'country',
    n: 'III',
    lines: ['Our', 'Country'],
    teaser: 'A National Council of the People on Immigration.',
    image: countryPanel,
    focus: '50% 70%',
  },
];

---

<Layout title="Be Part of It — We The People" introLines={['Be Part', 'of It']}>
  <PageHero lines={['Be Part', 'of It']} />

  <ReadProgress />
  <main>
    <p class="levels__intro">
      There are three ways to be part of it: in your life, in your community and across the
      country.
    </p>
    <p class="levels__intro levels__intro--more">
      As <strong>We The People</strong> grows, we’ll add more ways to get involved.
    </p>

    <nav class="levels" aria-label="Ways to be part of it">
      {
        LEVELS.map((l) => (
          <a class="level" href={`#${l.id}`} data-level={l.id}>
            <Image
              class="level__img"
              src={l.image}
              alt=""
              style={l.focus && `object-position:${l.focus}`}
              widths={[480, 960]}
              sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 50vw"
              format="webp"
              quality={70}
            />
            <span class="level__n">{l.n}</span>
            <span class="level__title">
              {l.lines.map((line, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && <br />}
                  {line}
                </>
              ))}
            </span>
            <span class="level__teaser">{l.teaser}</span>
            <span class="level__cta" aria-hidden="true">
              ↓
            </span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </nav>

    <!-- I: YOU ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="prose level-section" id="you">
      <Scene first src={tea} alt="Two people's hands around cups of tea on a small round table" kicker="I · You" heading="Can you #AgreeOnTea?" />

      <p class="lead">
        Even in these divided times, there is surely one last thing we Brits can agree on…
      </p>
      <p class="pull">Tea.</p>
      <p>
        Could you have a 10-minute cuppa with someone from a different political point of view
        to talk about a difficult topic?
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Perhaps with the family member that you’ve given up talking politics with.</li>
        <li>Your next-door neighbour who votes for that political party.</li>
        <li>Your workmate who doesn’t see the world your way at all.</li>
        <li>
          Or even that person you avoid in the street because they look like they belong to
          the other side.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you do, then in the time it takes your tea to cool, you’ll have helped the country
        cool down a little too.
      </p>
      <p>
        When <strong>We The People</strong> choose to talk and listen to each other with
        respect, we challenge the idea that our rifts are too wide and deep to bridge.
      </p>
      <p>
        And we might discover that those on the other side are more human and nuanced than the
        algorithm or our politicians and media want us to believe.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means there’s hope: we can come back together to find a way through this deeply
        difficult moment.
      </p>

      <h2>The Idea</h2>
      <p>
        The idea is to hold a respectful, honest conversation across the lines that divide us.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where both sides are free to speak and be heard; where any perspective can be
        expressed. Of course, it’s going to take courage. It’s going to take backbone.
      </p>
      <p>Even more so, because the topic for this tea is one of the most difficult we face:</p>
      <p class="pull">What do you think about immigration and how this country might approach it?</p>

      <h2>The Plan</h2>
      <p>Here’s how we suggest you go about it:</p>

      <section class="stage" aria-labelledby="stage-brew">
        <p class="stage__when">The Beginning</p>
        <h3 id="stage-brew">Gentle Brew</h3>
        <p>
          This first step is the most important. While you wait for the tea to brew, and add
          milk and sugar, make sure you check in as human beings first. How are you, what’s on
          your mind or going on in your life right now?
        </p>
        <p>
          Since so many of us have strong views about immigration, it’s good to connect as
          people before you dive in.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- the middle is the game itself: the rules as a card you could hold -->
      <section class="game-card" aria-labelledby="stage-dialogue">
        <header class="game-card__head">
          <p class="stage__when">The Middle</p>
          <h3 id="stage-dialogue">Dialogue</h3>
          <p class="game-card__meta">
            <span>2 people</span><span>3 rounds or more</span><span>90 seconds each</span>
          </p>
        </header>
        <p>It’s simple: each person has at least 3 rounds of 90 seconds each to speak.</p>
        <ol class="game-card__rules">
          <li>When one person speaks, the other must listen. No interruptions.</li>
          <li>
            When it’s your turn to speak, you give your honest and heartfelt point of view. No
            sugar-coating.
          </li>
          <li>
            When it’s your turn to listen, you really listen – rather than just thinking about
            what you’re going to say next.
          </li>
          <li>
            It’s best to use an alarm for timings so that you’re free to engage with the other
            person, not your phone.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="game-card__golden">
          <strong>The golden rule:</strong> treat the other person as you would want to be
          treated. Obviously that means be sure to avoid insults.
        </p>
        <p class="game-card__go">And if you want to do more than 3 rounds, go for it!</p>
      </section>

      <section class="stage" aria-labelledby="stage-reflect">
        <p class="stage__when">The End</p>
        <h3 id="stage-reflect">A Minute to Reflect</h3>
        <p>Once you both feel done, take one last minute to reflect on the process with each other.</p>
        <p>
          Don’t feel disheartened about how much you disagreed. It’s unlikely a single tea would
          change anyone’s minds!
        </p>
        <p>
          But did you glimpse a moment where you understood where the other was coming from? A
          moment when their tough opinion made more sense? Perhaps even an inch of common ground?
        </p>
        <p>If the journey to come back together is 10,000 miles, then you just took the first step.</p>
      </section>

      <h2>Spread the Word</h2>
      <p>
        If you feel comfortable, share a photo of your two hands, your cups or your mugs,
        tagging #AgreeOnTea and @WeThePeopleGather.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to go further you could share audio or video of the whole or part of the
        conversation. Make sure both parties are happy to do this before posting.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to keep it entirely private, that’s totally fine. The most important thing
        is that the conversation happens at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        But when others hear you did #AgreeOnTea, it might inspire them to do the same – and
        that’s how a bigger movement begins.
      </p>
      <p>
        By taking part in the conversation and spreading the message further we can all do our
        bit to show a more united, hopeful future is possible.
      </p>

      <!-- Other Ways (ADA-59). A green band until the client sends a licensed copy
           of its photograph (the one in the ticket is an iStock preview). -->
      <h2 class="band" id="other-ways">Other Ways</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        There are so many ways we as individuals can contribute to <strong>We The People</strong>
        in everyday life.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subhead">Through your work or local group</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>We The People</strong> know that the best way for us to reunite our country and
        transform our system is to collaborate with as many organisations and people as
        possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        That’s why we’re asking groups and organisations across Britain, big and small, to take
        a public stand with us and declare:
      </p>
      <ul class="pledge">
        <li>We are concerned that our country is deeply divided.</li>
        <li>We believe we can come back together.</li>
        <li>We commit to the principles: listening, respect and finding common ground.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This isn’t just for charities or advocacy groups but football clubs, book groups, your
        local church, your children’s school or GP surgery.
      </p>
      <p>
        Think about the groups and places where you belong and ask them to make a public pledge
        of support. Ask if they’d encourage members to #AgreeOnTea or consider starting a
        Council of the People.
      </p>
      <p>
        There might also be opportunities for stronger collaboration as the movement builds. If
        that interests your group, get in touch.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subhead">Get in touch &amp; spread the word</h3>
      <p>Have another idea for how you could help? Get in touch.</p>
      <p>
        We need people for organising, outreach, fundraising and, above all, for spreading the
        word.
      </p>
      <p>
        So share this website with three people in your life, and let them know a new approach
        for our country and our future is emerging.
      </p>
      <p>
        We know that this is an ambitious project that challenges our core beliefs about who we
        are, what we are capable of and how the world works.
      </p>
      <p>
        It’s bound to raise questions, doubts and disagreements, and we want to hear them all.
        The more voices that shape this movement, the stronger it will be.
      </p>
      <p>
        So get in touch and tell us what you think at
        <a href="mailto:hello@wethepeoplegather.com">hello@wethepeoplegather.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="subhead">Be the change</h3>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>“Be the change you want to see in the world”</p>
        <footer>Mahatma Gandhi</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        At the most basic level, if all of us who believe in the ideas of this movement could
        embody its principles a little more in daily life, we would start to lower the
        temperature in our country.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are an almost infinite number of ways we could do this. Just being kinder, more
        open and less confrontational would be a great start.
      </p>
      <p>You could try to listen better to the people in your own life. Your children, parents or colleagues.</p>
      <p>
        Be curious about other perspectives, rather than reducing people to labels. When you
        hear a point of view that you disagree with, try asking “What can they see that I’m not
        seeing?”
      </p>
      <p>
        Even just work on getting hold of your temper. See the person who bumped into you in the
        street or cut in front of you in the traffic as a human being, not an opponent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Of course, we’re only human, doing our best amid the pressures of modern life. We’re not
        asking you to become a saint.
      </p>
      <p>
        But every time you choose to listen instead of shout back, when you don’t rush to judge
        or condemn someone and instead treat them as a human worthy of respect, you do your bit
        to help Britain find its way back together.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- II: YOUR COMMUNITY ------------------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="prose level-section" id="community">
      <Scene first src={community} alt="A woman laughing with her hands over her mouth, talking with friends at a table" kicker="II · Your Community" heading="We The People of…" headingMore="Dover. Brixton. Edinburgh. Piddington. York. Cardiff. Hastings — and more." focus="50% 40%" />

      <p>
        The issue of immigration has touched many of our communities, for better or worse. Our
        political rifts find their way into our day-to-day lives and the places we call home.
      </p>
      <p>
        As our movement grows, <strong>We The People</strong> will hold gatherings in villages,
        towns and cities for larger groups of people (from 20–100) to help whole communities
        talk to each other again with honesty and respect and to find a way through our deep
        divides.
      </p>
      <p>We call these gatherings a <strong>Council of the People</strong>.</p>
      <p>
        In essence, the Councils are expanded versions of the one-on-one conversations and based
        on the same principles: everyone is welcome and every point of view can be spoken and
        heard freely, without fear.
      </p>
      <p>The central question remains:</p>
      <p class="pull">What do you think about immigration and how this country might approach it?</p>

      <!-- a solid green band, out to the viewport edges like a <Scene>, carrying
           the turn to the two paths below -->
      <p class="paths-banner">
        We offer two paths to hold a Council of the People for your community, whatever its
        size.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="path-n">1.</span> Let us do it</h2>
      <p>
        We can do it for you, creating a bespoke programme and approach tailored to your
        community and the unique ways in which questions around immigration have affected it.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also offer impartial facilitation for the Council itself. That means trained members
        of our team will be present in person to support the conversation and ensure the
        <strong>We The People</strong> principles are honoured: that all voices and perspectives
        are heard and treated with respect.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we do not have a team available for your Council, we will try to connect you with a
        trusted local facilitation team. Everyone we recommend has been vetted to ensure they
        share our commitment to impartiality.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our support is free but you’ll need to find an appropriate venue, provide refreshments
        and take the lead in promoting the event locally.
      </p>

      <h2><span class="path-n">2.</span> Do it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        If demand means we cannot provide direct support, or if you prefer to run the event
        yourself, we’ll soon have a free toolkit available for download which offers a clear
        step-by-step process, principles and resources to hold a gathering yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        If no-one in your group feels comfortable running the event, we can connect you with a
        vetted facilitator in your area for in-person support on the day.
        <a href="mailto:hello@wethepeoplegather.com">Get in touch</a> for more information.
      </p>

      <p class="pull">
        The dream: to inspire a wave of reconnection and reconciliation in communities across
        the country.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- III: OUR COUNTRY --------------------------------------------------- -->
    <article class="prose level-section" id="country">
      <Scene first src={council} alt="A large circular debating chamber, rows of blue seats rising round a central floor" kicker="III · Our Country" heading="A National Council of the People" />

      <p class="lead">
        Our individual conversations and community work will build towards a major cultural
        event: a National Council of the People on Immigration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since we can’t gather the whole country, we will use a lottery-based model to pick
        participants. A group of 100 to 150 everyday people will be selected. We’ll ensure the
        group reflects the population as a whole based on factors like class, age, gender, race
        and political perspective.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over a period of up to two weeks, the Council will hear testimony from experts on
        immigration as well as personal stories of those whose lives have been affected by the
        issue.
      </p>

      <Scene src={councilTalk} alt="A small group of men of different ages talking over drinks, one of them speaking with his hands" focus="50% 35%" />
      <p>
        Clearly, this subject is incredibly volatile. But we believe the National Council of the
        People must allow all perspectives to be aired, even those that either side might find
        extreme, to make sure that it truly represents our country as a whole.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the Council will hear the case for a more open approach to immigration. That could
        include the legacy of immigration in shaping modern Britain and the contribution
        migrants make today.
      </p>
      <p>
        And they will hear the case for more immigration control, including those who want to
        reverse it. That could include concerns about the pace of change in communities, the
        impact on culture and traditions, or crime and safety.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inevitably this conversation will be about what it means to be British. What does it
        mean to belong here? What are our core British values and how do they apply to those
        who want to come here and stay?
      </p>
      <p>
        They will explore how the debate itself has affected us all. For example, concerns that
        it fuels racism towards ethnic minority communities, including people born and raised
        here. Or that people feel unable to voice legitimate concerns without suffering social
        or legal repercussions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Like every conversation and the local Councils of the People before it, this gathering
        will be a place where all sides can speak without fear of judgment. Where all
        perspectives will be heard. Where ghosts from the past can be faced. Where we can
        express anger, grief, even shed tears.
      </p>
      <p>
        The crucial difference is that the National Council of the People will bring
        representatives from the whole country into one room. Voices from every region and every
        walk of life, across the full spectrum of opinion finally coming together face to face.
      </p>
      <p>This is how we, as a people, can finally find our way through this issue together.</p>

      <Scene src={impact} alt="A packed stadium at night, a green stage of hills and chimneys lit up in the middle" heading="A Moment of National Cultural Impact" />
      <p>
        Even though most of us will not take part directly, there will be loads of ways to
        follow along and get involved in the National Council of the People, whoever and
        wherever you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        Just as the Jubilee sparked street parties across the country, we hope the National
        Council will spark conversation about immigration across the country, one rooted in
        honesty and respect.
      </p>
      <p>
        There will be an app where you can follow what’s happening in the Council, post comments
        and vote on the ideas that resonate most with you personally.
      </p>
      <p>
        There will also be a portal where you can submit your own stories or perspective. We’ll
        all have a chance to speak without fear of judgment and to contribute.
      </p>
      <p>
        And we’ll encourage everyone from everyday people to public figures across the arts,
        music, film and TV to share their points of view. Some may be submitted for
        consideration in the Council. Together, our stories will stand as a collection of
        national memory on immigration.
      </p>

      <Scene src={policy} alt="Children holding up a Union flag together outdoors" heading="The People’s Policy" focus="50% 40%" />
      <p>
        At the end of this process, we will have the first policy on immigration written by the
        people ourselves, not politicians.
      </p>
      <p>
        Of course, there will be hard compromises. Undoubtedly everyone will find some part hard
        to stomach. Yet it will be the first policy ever put forward on this issue that most
        people can accept. A policy in all our names.
      </p>
      <p>
        This will be a breakthrough in how we do democracy. One that will be impossible to
        ignore. A prototype for a new political system.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .levels__intro {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 10vh, 6rem) 1.5rem clamp(2.5rem, 7vh, 4rem);
    font-size: var(--type-lg);
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-align: center;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: rgba(246, 244, 238, 0.86);
  }
  .levels__intro:has(+ .levels__intro--more) {
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }
  .levels__intro--more {
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }
  .levels__intro strong {
    color: #fff;
  }

  /* --- the three levels ---------------------------------------------------- */
  .levels {
    display: flex;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-wtp-bramble);
  }
  .level {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    min-height: 58vh;
    padding: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--color-wtp-bramble);
    transition:
      flex-grow 450ms var(--ease-out),
      background-color 300ms ease;
  }
  .level:last-child {
    border-right: 0;
  }
  /* the photograph sits under a scrim, and comes up as the panel is chosen */
  .level__img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0.22;
    transition: opacity 450ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  .level__n {
    font-family: var(--font-wordmark);
    font-size: var(--type-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    color: var(--color-wtp-dawn);
  }
  .level__title {
    font-family: var(--font-wordmark);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: var(--type-display-lg);
    line-height: 0.95;
    color: #fff;
  }
  .level__teaser {
    max-width: 26ch;
    font-size: var(--type-base);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(246, 244, 238, 0.78);
  }
  .level__cta {
    margin-top: auto;
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    color: var(--color-wtp-dawn);
  }
  .level:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-wtp-dawn);
    outline-offset: -4px;
  }

  /* Hover widens the chosen level and brings its line in. Touch devices get
     every line up front — there is no hover to reveal it. */
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .level__teaser {
      opacity: 0;
      translate: 0 8px;
      transition:
        opacity 300ms var(--ease-out),
        translate 300ms var(--ease-out);
    }
    .level:hover,
    .level:focus-visible {
      flex-grow: 2;
    }
    .level:hover .level__img,
    .level:focus-visible .level__img {
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
    .level:hover .level__teaser,
    .level:focus-visible .level__teaser {
      opacity: 1;
      translate: 0 0;
      transition-delay: 120ms;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .levels {
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    .level {
      min-height: auto;
      border-right: 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-wtp-bramble);
    }
    .level:last-child {
      border-bottom: 0;
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .level,
    .level__teaser {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* --- sections ------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .level-section {
    scroll-margin-top: 0; /* each opens on its photograph, under the header */
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  /* the two-paths banner: solid green, out to the viewport edges like a
     <Scene>, its words kept in the column */
  .prose .paths-banner {
    margin: 3rem calc(50% - 50vw);
    padding: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 5.5rem) calc(50vw - 50%);
    background: var(--color-wtp-green);
    color: #fff;
    font-size: var(--type-xl);
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .path-n {
    color: var(--color-wtp-dawn);
  }

  /* Other Ways opens on a green band, like the paths banner, its heading in
     the column */
  .prose h2.band {
    margin: 4.5rem calc(50% - 50vw) 2.2rem;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 5.5rem) calc(50vw - 50%);
    background: var(--color-wtp-green);
    font-size: var(--type-display-xl);
    line-height: 0.98;
    scroll-margin-top: 0;
  }
  /* small indented heads within a section: Anton caps behind a dawn rule */
  .prose h3.subhead {
    margin: 3rem 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-wtp-dawn);
    font-family: var(--font-wordmark);
    font-weight: 400;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-size: var(--type-display-sm);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: #fff;
  }
  /* the pledge a group makes, as its own statement */
  .prose .pledge {
    margin: 1.4rem 0 1.6rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem 1.2rem 2.6rem;
    background: var(--color-wtp-green);
    color: #fff;
  }
  .prose .pledge li::before {
